WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.120
My history is your history,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.880
your history is my history,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.720
and our history is our story.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.400
We share the same place
in time and space

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.440
in the world, in today's society,
and we owe it to each other

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.640
to know more about
our ancestors together.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.360
If we want to see the legacies

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.120
of slavery and colonialism
in Britain,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.560
we need look no further
than the people.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:37.320
So my father left Jamaica in 1958,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.880
he came here to live with
his sister and to find work.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.160
He found that experience, as
you can imagine, quite daunting.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.160
He often reminds me of seeing
smoke coming out of a chimney

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.720
and him thinking that
the house was on fire.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.080
So he settled here and,
eighteen months later,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.720
he sent for my
mother and my sister.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.920
Jen Reid grew up in Bath,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.320
the only black child at school, where
she regularly encountered racism.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:10.880
I moved to Bristol in 2012.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.800
You know, I've always been
very aware of what Bristol is,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.200
and it being built
on the back of slavery.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.600
I remember an office
that I had worked in,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.800
directly opposite was Colston
standing proud over Bristol.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.560
The statue that Jen is referring
to was of Edward Colston -

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.640
a 17th Century Bristol
merchant and slave trader.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.760
He was a member of the
Royal African Company.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.280
Between 1662 and 1731,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:47.440
the company transported 212,000
enslaved people across the Atlantic,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.320
of whom 44,000 died en route.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.040
As well as a statue
in the city centre,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.560
schools, streets, and many
other buildings in Bristol

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.360
were named after Edward Colston.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.840
When he died he left a lot
of his wealth to charities in Bristol

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:04.760
and it's for that reason

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.400
why his name was registered
very largely on the city,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.520
but his larger role

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.840
in the trading of Africans

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.720
for slavery and slave labour

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.120
in the Caribbean

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.360
is something that is very
important for us to emphasise

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.080
because it speaks to a silence
that often follows

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.040
a lot of these figures in history who
were involved in the slavery business

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.080
and how that silence is obscured
by this elevation of them

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.640
in these other roles that
they performed in their lives.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.600
But on the 7th of June 2020,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.360
in the midst of global protests
against racism,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.280
sparked in part by the murder
of George Floyd

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.000
by an American policeman,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.520
protestors in Bristol tore Colston
down from his elevated status,

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.000
both literally and figuratively.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.760
Jen Reid and her husband,
Alasdair Doggart, were there -

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.960
attending their first ever
political protest.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.880
It was a sight to behold, it was
almost like a carnival atmosphere.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.360
I watched my husband drag
Edward Colston to the water

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:13.640
and throw him in to the water.
It was just such an historic moment.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.520
It was an instinctive action
on my behalf.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.400
As soon as I put my hands on him,

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:22.000
and we started pushing
him towards the water,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.520
then it was as simple as:

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.520
"I know how to do that,
I can do that. Let's do it".

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.640
Jen's role in the protest renewed her
interest in tracing her family tree.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:35.480
With the help of the Legacies
of Slavery database at UCL,

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:39.120
she was finally able to find out
more about her family in Jamaica.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.160
What really shook me to the core

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:47.640
was discovering that my great-great-
great-great-great-grandmother

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.480
was the first of my ancestors
to be enslaved.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.560
Jen also discovered

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.240
that the documents relating
to her 5x great-grandmother

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:01.640
had listed her name as Sylvia.
But that wasn’t her real name.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.840
Like many enslaved people,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.280
she’d been given an English name
by her enslaver -

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.920
robbing her of her African identity.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.480
I’ve also discovered that my
grandmother five times removed

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.720
had a daughter.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.320
And her daughter had a daughter.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.720
And they were all kept
together by their enslaver,

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.720
by the name of Donald McLean.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.800
It made me really angry.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.360
It made me want to research
the McLean clan -

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.960
who are they, where are they now
what riches do they have?

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:43.600
A lot of those memories
of slavery are not memories

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.080
that are just recorded
and registered in history books

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.280
or that are told in classes.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.360
These are memories
that people embody.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.720
They are memories that people carry
from generation to generation.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.280
For Jen’s husband Alasdair,
the Black Lives Matter protests

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.200
also triggered an interest
in finding about his past.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.040
When he started looking
into his family history,

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.200
he found that he was related
to people with the surname Colston -

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:08.880
though there’s no evidence

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.240
that they were connected
to Edward Colston himself.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:14.280
I think it's a complex picture

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.080
when you try to unpick
the detail of everything

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.680
about why race
is so important today,

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.320
and why people have
to accept the truth

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.720
of Britain's involvement
in the slave trade.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.800
I think history
can't be sectioned off

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.480
and it's “this is your history,
this is my history”.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.000
It's our history together.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.000
As well as personal connections to
the slave traders and the enslaved,

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.680
in Britain there are
deep financial ones.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.440
When slavery in Britain
was abolished in the 1830s,

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.600
the government paid £20 million
to slave owners in compensation.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.680
There was no compensation
for the formerly enslaved.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.320
The compensation of £20 million

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.400
was really meant to be a form
of reparations to slave owners

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.600
for the loss of their
property and people.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.280
An extraordinary figure, and that
even as we consider today

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:06.920
the £20 million,

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.320
we should also remember
that it is a fraction

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.440
of the full extent of the wealth
earned by enslaved labour

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.000
for Great Britain in the entire
period of British slavery.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.120
A lot of that money ended up
being reinvested

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:23.000
in banking, in finance,
in the commercial sector,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.560
but also in
non-commercial activities,

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.520
in the arts, and in museums

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.280
and even in education

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.840
and philanthropic
associations and endeavours.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:34.640
So there’s clearly a disconnect

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.520
between the wealth
that slavery brought to Britain

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.400
and the immense suffering inflicted
on those who were enslaved.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.880
In some cases, it’s only names
that can offer clues to the past.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.000
There are threads that are connected
to places and to place names.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.080
Why a plantation in Eastern Jamaica

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.520
shares the name
of a location in London,

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.320
but there are also threads
that are connected to people.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.520
Why someone in Bridgetown, Barbados,

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.480
shares the name
of somebody in Liverpool

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.680
even though they have never met.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.560
They are tied to that history

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.120
through the history
of slavery and colonialism,

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.440
and it is something
that we cannot ignore.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.520
As for Jen, although she knows the
names of her family’s enslaver,

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:18.120
she still doesn’t know her ancestor
Sylvia’s real name,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.960
or anything about
her origins in Africa.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:24.080
I want to know what tribe
I belong to,

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:28.440
I imagine my tribe to be fearless
because I am fearless,

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:29.960
and I am powerful,

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:33.400
and I imagine my ancestors
to be exactly the same.

